{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793009386,"nonce":"v/tAac9qjwiIbUQZYkVAjfkl3cg=","title":"Cloud Sync: move a Git workspace between desktop and browser","message":"Product intent\nCloud Sync is a separate built-in sidebar tool for a developer's durable personal or shared cloud workspace. Use Remix upload-cloud-2-fill (Icons.UPLOAD_CLOUD_2_FILL), already available in ashui. It reuses account, session, CRDT, storage and sandbox services; starting, stopping or expiring Cloud Sync does not implicitly start or stop a Jam. A Jam can later attach only through an explicit workspace grant. Local editing and Git remain account-free.\n\nRequirements and defaults\n- The source is a Git working tree and its owner has a rayde.org account. A GitHub remote is not required: local-only repositories, other Git hosts, linked worktrees and an unborn repository are valid. GitHub checkout remains a separate optional import path.\n- Default workspace storage allocation is 1 GB (1,000,000,000 bytes), enforced by the service. The owner's aggregate quota, workspace count, upload rate and compute budget also apply so creating more workspaces cannot bypass limits.\n- Default cloud expiry is 24 hours from the first successfully published sync, measured by server time. Show its absolute time and time remaining. Owners may choose shorter times and any longer duration their entitlement allows. Viewing, restarting compute or silently retrying a sync must not extend retention. Renewal is explicit.\n- Paid accounts may select retained storage allocations of different sizes. Proposed catalog sizes for cost validation: 1, 10, 50 and 100 GB. These are design options, not approved prices or launch allowances. Retained storage can outlive compute and remain until explicitly deleted or the disclosed subscription/retention policy applies. Do not promise unlimited storage or always-on compute.\n- A successfully persisted initial sync resolves to https://app.rayde.org/{workspace_id}, with Copy Link and Open in Browser. Reuse the current short workspace route; the stable workspace identity survives sandbox restarts and is distinct from an ephemeral compute lease or login session. The URL contains no credentials. A copied URL grants only the current sharing policy.\n\nSidebar experience\nBefore eligibility: show the workspace name, Sign In or Initialize Git as appropriate, and the reason Sync is unavailable. Initializing Git is a separate user action, never a hidden side effect of Sync. Eligible preflight shows branch, proposed included files/size, exclusions, storage allocation, expiry (24 hours selected), and a primary Sync button.\n\nUse explicit states: Not Synced, Checking, Uploading (bytes/progress), Verifying, Synced, Offline/Changes Pending, Conflict, Failed/Retry, Expiring, Expired and Deleting. Separate storage sync status from compute Starting/Running/Sleeping. Keep the sidebar lightweight with a virtualized file/conflict list, bounded status updates, ash-future/background work, cancellation and event-driven progress. Closed/signed-out tools add no idle polling.\n\nAfter initial publication show the copyable URL, last durable sync time, storage usage/limit, expiry and Share. Browser and desktop edits then continue bidirectionally; this is workspace sync, not a one-time ZIP upload. Allow Pause Sync, Sync Now, Download/Export, Extend Expiry when permitted, Disconnect This Device, and Delete Cloud Copy. Disconnect and cloud deletion never remove local source files. Newly edited excluded files are marked Local Only. The account's Cloud Workspaces list provides Open/Resume on another device.\n\nWhat is synchronized\nPreserve working-tree content, unsaved editor buffers, selected untracked files, tracked deletes, branch/HEAD, local unpushed commits and reconstructable staged versus unstaged state. Use a normalized Git object/ref export plus file/index manifests; never upload .git wholesale. Support linked worktrees without following their .git pointer outside the selected tree. An unborn repository has a file/index snapshot without requiring a bundle. A sync does not push, commit, reset, checkout or rewrite the user's local repository. The local machine can go offline after publication; browser work uses cloud authority and synchronizes back on reconnect.\n\nRespect ignore rules for untracked files and allow explicit selection. Generated output and package/LSP/editor caches stay excluded by default. Credentials, private keys, auth-bearing Git config, hooks, credential helpers, .env secrets, RayDE session state and OS files are excluded or require a clear explicit file decision; tracked status does not prove a file is safe. Preview omissions before first sync. Git history itself can contain secrets: explicitly disclose whether the selected history is included; offer a working-tree-only cloud Git snapshot, and do not claim secret detection is exhaustive. Selective/history-free export reconstructs Git state only to the extent its selected base permits; the UI must describe that tradeoff.\n\nSubmodules and Git LFS need explicit manifests and authorized content transfer. Do not silently dereference submodules, symlinks, external classpaths or local dependency mounts. In-workspace symlinks and executable modes have a defined portable representation; unsafe links and case/path collisions are reported before publication. Rebuildable dependencies are installed into the sandbox under resource policy. A build needing excluded local dependencies reports that requirement rather than uploading arbitrary outside directories.\n\nConsistency and transfer\nCapture a consistent local generation across Git/index/files/open buffers, fence concurrent Git operations, and revalidate changed files while scanning. Do not freeze the UI or copy the whole workspace into memory. Hash and stream bounded chunks; reuse already-owned blobs, reserve quota before transfer, retry/resume interrupted parts and garbage-collect abandoned uploads. Object ownership/deduplication is scoped to the billing owner/workspace, with no cross-user existence oracle.\n\nPublish the new manifest atomically only after its chunks, Git export, checksum and size checks are durable. Failed/cancelled uploads never replace the last valid generation. Return the URL/mark Synced only when that generation and its acknowledged document updates survive a compute kill. Compute can wake lazily on Open in Browser with separate readiness feedback. Directory changes, binary versions, Git operations and manifest publication use ordered revisions and compare-and-swap against the base generation.\n\nReuse Yjs/Yrs for text edits, with stable document identity across rename/move. Use the existing common sync path for online desktop and browser clients rather than a competing cloud file writer. Persist acknowledged CRDT updates/checkpoints under workspace authority; compact bounded logs into R2 checkpoints. Reconcile an offline filesystem edit against its recorded base and merge text where possible. Preserve both binary/conflicting versions and show a resolve/diff action; never silently overwrite cloud or local work. Git ref/index changes are serialized and surfaced as conflicts when incompatible. Build/Run uses a persisted revision/flush barrier and reports which revision ran.\n\nArchitecture and ownership\n1. Rust Worker API: authenticate RayDE identities, handle desktop external-browser PKCE sign-in, authorize CRUD/sharing/transfer/restore, and stream bounded object transfers. Existing GitHub App credentials stay server-side and are not required for a desktop-origin upload. Extend the account directory rather than treating a transient OAuth session or email as workspace ownership.\n2. Workspace Durable Object + SQLite: stable owner, members, sharing policy and ACL version, source/import type, storage allocation, expiresAt/retained policy, current manifest generation, document journal/checkpoint refs, sync jobs and a fenced compute lease. A separate account usage authority serializes aggregate reservations; D1 can project searchable account/workspace listings. Persist meaningful transitions, not a billing row per keystroke.\n3. Private R2 storage: chunks, safe Git data, versioned manifests, document checkpoints and export objects. A product workspace bucket is a quota/namespace, normally a private account/workspace prefix, not a newly provisioned physical R2 bucket for each user workspace. Never buffer a 1 GB workspace in a Durable Object. The initial transfer service enforces streamed length/hash/lease checks. Direct presigned uploads are optional only after proving exact bounds, quota reservations and safe abandoned-object cleanup; they never authorize publication. Private downloads are authorized at the gateway for prompt revocation.\n4. Rust sandbox provisioner/broker: extend the existing runner seam with desktop snapshot sources, materialize a durable revision into one MSB environment per active workspace, start the Rust session, LSP, extensions and PTY, and reconcile watched changes through the same revision/document authority. The VM disk is disposable materialized state/cache, not the only copy of acknowledged edits. Restart from checkpoints and safe Git data with no local machine or GitHub token required. Use short-lived credentials, private authenticated ingress, network/resource limits and epoch fencing to prevent two active writers. Keep the provider replaceable.\n\nMSB runs on a suitable external host through the provider; a Durable Object controls it rather than running its VMM. The NUC's existing local MSB isolation probe is a development starting point, not a deployed production provider. The current gateway already has authenticated workspace routing and idempotent provisioning, but is owner-only, takes repository checkout input, and relays to a Rust process whose document authority is still local. Desktop upload/restore, ACLs, retention/quota enforcement, a production runner and durable document authority remain implementation work. Critical services stay Rust; JavaScript is browser/SDK glue and test tooling only.\n\nSharing\nDefault General Access is Restricted. Share supports named RayDE users plus an optional Anyone with the link setting; all participants must sign in to a RayDE account in the initial release. A link policy explicitly selects Viewer or Editor and can be disabled/rotated. Anonymous public viewing is a separate future opt-in, not implied by the workspace URL. Show who can access and who pays; guests do not need a paid plan, and usage belongs to the workspace's explicit personal/team billing owner.\n\nOwner manages membership, link policy, expiry, allocation, deletion and ownership. Editor changes files/documents and Git state, with no ACL/billing/deletion authority. Viewer reads authorized source/history/previews and cannot edit, write files, spawn tools, use a terminal, install extensions or invoke mutating Git operations. Build/Run, terminal and extension execution are separate grants, defaulting to owner-only; show owners that an editor can modify code later executed by an authorized user. Do not auto-run untrusted newly shared work on open. Sharing a Git remote is not a prerequisite for a snapshot invite; Git credentials and push permission are separate and never inherited from workspace membership.\n\nEnforce roles at every document, filesystem, Git, process, extension, asset and export operation in both gateway and session; hiding buttons or accepting client readOnly flags is insufficient. Refactor the current owner-only guard into policy checks. Invitations are scoped, expiring, revocable, bound to the intended RayDE identity when named, and safely claimed after sign-in. Membership/role revocation advances the ACL version, closes or downgrades existing sockets, invalidates outstanding publish/execution leases and blocks reconnect. Old offline clients cannot replay mutations after revocation or expiry; they may keep their own local unsynced recovery copy. Read-only opens must not create arbitrary durable document state.\n\nExpiry, deletion and cost\nCheck expiresAt and current policy on every admission/mutation/read, independently of background jobs. At expiry, deny cloud access, fence writes, close sockets and previews, terminate compute/PTY/extensions and revoke transfer leases. Durable Object alarms drive idempotent cleanup with a durable due-work schedule (sharing the one alarm with other deadlines), plus a reconciler for exhausted retries/outages. R2 lifecycle rules are a coarse backstop for abandoned data, not exact workspace authorization deadlines. They typically remove expired objects within 24 hours and have a bounded rule count; do not allocate a rule per workspace.\n\nDelete cloud Git/files/checkpoints/exports and unused staging uploads, destroy provider disks/snapshots, remove sharing grants and account index entries, then release reservations/storage charges after physical-object accounting. Keep a minimal deletion tombstone/idempotency record with a documented bounded operational retention. Resource leases have independent hard limits so a lost control plane cannot leave compute running forever. Workspace expiry, login expiry, invitation expiry, compute idle sleep and paid storage retention are separate clocks. Extension/renewal races with cleanup need versioned tombstones so old jobs cannot delete a renewed generation. Account deletion cascades through the same idempotent lifecycle; no local files are removed.\n\nStorage allowance counts retained referenced blob/Git/checkpoint bytes and in-flight reservations across the owner, not just the latest source tree. Also cap expanded live size, single-file size, file count, history, update logs, decompression and bandwidth/rate so compression or a small manifest cannot bypass budgets. Show usage and a preflight over-limit decision before upload. Pause new cloud writes at quota exhaustion with export/local recovery available; do not silently upgrade or charge. Compute minutes, concurrency, CPU/RAM/disk, egress and storage are separate budgets. Idle compute sleeps while retained data persists. Downgrade/payment-failure grace and export/deletion times are explicit versioned entitlements, with no immediate surprise deletion or automatic retained-storage purchase.\n\nImplementation order and release gates\nA. Reuse account/credential and entitlement tracks; define workspace IDs, roles, source types, generation/lease format, quota units and expiry semantics. Add policy and protocol fixtures first.\nB. Implement sidebar preflight plus a bounded resumable Git snapshot upload and atomic R2 publication, with account quotas and expiry checks enabled before any hosted pilot. Return a real copyable authorized URL.\nC. Extend the MSB provider on the NUC and local Worker harness: boot from the uploaded snapshot, edit from another browser/device, kill/recreate compute, restore and sync back to desktop. Prove all acknowledged edits survive without the originating desktop. No production deployment is implicit.\nD. Complete named/link sharing, server-enforced roles and active revocation; validate invite flow and unauthorized RPC attempts in real clients.\nE. Add paid retained allocations via the shared billing/entitlement catalog, expiry/reclamation/account deletion, fault and resource tests, and a cost review before choosing prices/launch limits.\n\nAcceptance harness covers dirty staged/unstaged/untracked/unborn/local-only Git repos, unsaved buffers, linked worktrees, LFS/submodules, excluded secrets/history choice, unsafe symlinks and case collisions; upload cancellation/restart/checksum/quota races; simultaneous desktop/browser edits and file rename/delete; compute/DO restart and duplicate alarms; expiry/renewal/delete races; revoked/viewer direct RPC and offline replay; account isolation, long idle periods, memory/backpressure and storage/compute billing separation. Run the existing browser/session/CRDT/LSP suites. After code milestones rebuild WASM and session services for user testing. This issue records a plan only, not shipped cloud functionality.\n\nExisting work reused\n- Accounts: 92e2f4ec084fb6cc60b98b95b8c86cc27b63277a3393f241402ecbf676fb4268\n- Account ownership: 47b9f21def8e4305662dce79184792213d1cb96486e3e5036f1863b91f752f90\n- Cloud gateway: 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904\n- MSB/session execution: 670bd15d5f2a4c8fdd82da62e9b72599f9a689affc5682a7b621f81fd72156c0\n- Session resource limits: 080ea85416efa648d8a634f8a1906bdecb0e70bf9da9cf934675d832d8533081\n- Entitlements: 7ed57829f2163d4b44f248e0a4786526a2a702d04c50efa6ed8d037ebcd74b22\n- Account/tier plan: f932d13f68b6e7ac0f0cf40998b15e209c90bacbce6b5b3b594ed67995f125ba\nCloud Sync is a separate product/resource policy; its 24-hour default does not replace Jam's independently proposed retention.\n\nPrimary references checked 2026-10-26\n- https://docs.microsandbox.dev/getting-started/introduction — runtime/SDK/microVM boundary.\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/ — one alarm per actor, at-least-once delivery, bounded automatic retries.\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/buckets/object-lifecycles/ — asynchronous physical expiry and rule constraints.\n- https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/s3/presigned-urls/ — temporary per-operation/object access, separate from application membership.","files":null}]}